ashley: Quick report from a customer onboarding on Vercel Edge: when they consume our ESM package the SDK's generic overloads collapse and consumers see `any`. Repro: https://github.com/redwood/repro-edge-types-minimal

rafael: ouch. smallest repro?

ashley: gist below — same pattern we've seen: overloaded declaration, single impl. works in CJS, breaks after esm packaging.
```ts
// packages/sdk/src/api.ts
export function send<T>(x: T): Promise<{ value: T }>;
export function send(x: unknown) { return Promise.resolve({ value: x as any }); }
```
consumer:
```ts
import { send } from '@redwood/sdk';
type P = { id: string };
async function t(){
  const r = await send<P>({ id: '1' });
  // r.value -> expected P; in ESM edge bundle it's any
}
```

sue: double-check the package.json exports/types. If exports.import points at a bundle whose .d.ts was generated by a bundler that flattens declarations, TS will pick the flattened d.ts. Also watch typesVersions.

kevin: short fixes: 1) point import export to compiled file with preserved d.ts, 2) run api-extractor/dts-bundle to produce a curated index.d.ts with explicit overloads, or 3) ship a hand-authored index.d.ts for the public API.

rafael: we should add a CI smoke that installs the published tarball into a tiny edge fixture and runs `npx tsc --noEmit` to catch this. also emit declarations for both cjs and esm and diff them.

types-bot: Created ticket RW-DX-8342: "ESM packaging collapses SDK overloads for Edge consumers" (triaged).

ashley: i'll add the vercel fixture + CI check and attach the repro. thanks. :thumbsup:
